\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{An odd spinor action selects one Clifford summand}

\p{Work over #{\mathbb C} with
#{V=W\oplus W'\oplus\mathbb C z}, where #{W,W'} are polar-dual isotropic
spaces of dimension #{\ell}, #{z} is orthogonal to them, and #{Q(z)=1}.
On #{S=\bigwedge W}, the two Clifford actions agree on #{W\oplus W'} and
differ on the last generator:

##{\rho_+(\iota_Q(z))=\alpha,\qquad
 \rho_-(\iota_Q(z))=-\alpha,}

where #{\alpha} is exterior parity. For polar-dual bases, normalize the
hyperbolic chirality element by

##{\Gamma=\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}
 \bigl(1-2\iota_Q(e_i)\iota_Q(e_i')\bigr).}

The even-dimensional action sends #{\Gamma} to
#{\prod_i(1-2N_i)=\alpha}. Moreover #{\Gamma^2=1}, and #{\Gamma}
anticommutes with every hyperbolic vector. Consequently

##{\chi=\Gamma\iota_Q(z),\qquad
 \chi^2=1}

is central in #{\Cl(Q)}. The complementary central idempotents

##{p_+=\frac{1+\chi}{2},\qquad
 p_-=\frac{1-\chi}{2}}

satisfy

##{\rho_+(p_+)=1,\quad \rho_+(p_-)=0,\qquad
 \rho_-(p_+)=0,\quad \rho_-(p_-)=1.}

Thus each odd action has a visible nonzero kernel.}

\p{The central idempotents also identify the whole algebra, rather than only
showing a dimension mismatch. Given
#{A\in\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(S)}, the preceding theorem supplies a
hyperbolic Clifford element #{a} with common image #{A}. Therefore the joint
map obeys

##{(\rho_+,\rho_-)(p_+a)=(A,0),\qquad
 (\rho_+,\rho_-)(p_-a)=(0,A).}

It is surjective. Since

##{\dim_{\mathbb C}\Cl(Q)=2^{2\ell+1}
 =2\dim_{\mathbb C}\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(S),}

it is an isomorphism

##{\Cl(Q)\simeq
 \operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(S)\oplus
 \operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(S).}

The corresponding abstract two-block existence theorem is
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.nonempty_algEquiv_matrix_prod_of_finrank_eq_two_mul_add_one}.
The explicit joint-map proof determines how the two actions select its
summands.}

\p{The restrictions #{\rho_+|_{\Cl(Q)^0}} and
#{\rho_-|_{\Cl(Q)^0}} are parity-conjugate. When #{\ell\geq1} they are
unequal, as the Spin element below shows; for #{\ell=0} both are the same
scalar action. For every even Clifford word, the number of hyperbolic factors
has the same parity as the number of #{z}-factors. Conjugation by #{\alpha}
contributes exactly that sign, so

##{\rho_-(x)=\alpha\rho_+(x)\alpha^{-1}
 \qquad(x\in\Cl(Q)^0).}

If #{\rho_+(x)=0}, conjugacy makes both coordinates of the joint isomorphism
zero, hence #{x=0}. The even source and target have the same dimension, so
either restriction is an isomorphism

##{\Cl(Q)^0\simeq\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(S).}

This faithful range is formalized by
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.evenSpinAction_surjective} and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.evenCliffordEquivEnd}.}

\p{When #{\ell\geq1}, exterior parity is not a decomposition into invariant
odd-dimensional Spin modules. Fix #{i}, put #{u=e_i+e_i'}, and note
#{Q(u)=Q(z)=1}. The even product

##{g=\iota_Q(z)\iota_Q(u)\in\Spin(Q)}

belongs to the actual Spin carrier by the norm-one pair criterion
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.ι_mul_ι_mem_spinGroup_of_norm_mul_norm_eq_one}.
Its plus action is #{\alpha(c_i+a_i)} and satisfies

##{1\longmapsto-e_i,\qquad e_i\longmapsto1.}

Its minus action is the negative of this operator. Thus the two even
restrictions are unequal, and this actual Spin element exchanges even and
odd exterior degree. For #{\ell=0}, the carrier #{S} is one-dimensional and
#{S^-=0}; no two-summand parity claim is made.}

\p{\citet{Lemma 20.16 and (20.18), p. 306}{fulton2004representation}
give the odd complex product algebra and the faithful even restriction. Their
second full-Clifford module is written on #{\bigwedge W'}, rather than as a
second sign on the same carrier.}

\p{\citet{Theorem 6.1.3, proof, pp. 304--305}{goodman2009symmetry}
construct the exterior carrier.
\citet{Lemma 6.1.4(3), p. 306}{goodman2009symmetry}
put the two normalized odd-vector signs on this common carrier. This supplies
the common-carrier translation used above.}

\p{\citet{Section 3.2.4, (3.5), p. 60}{meinrenken2013clifford}
supplies the normalized chirality product on the hyperbolic block. His
Theorem 3.3 is an even split theorem, not an odd product theorem. The
two-idempotent conclusion here is restricted to the split complex setting;
an odd Clifford center over a general real field need not split.}
